#{[17592186360581 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360581 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95171" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360581 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360581 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $u(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xyz,$ find $\\frac{∂u}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360581 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f89a4f6-9145-5fdf-84a8-9fcaed162c54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360581 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $u(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂u}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂u}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂u}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $u$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂z}=2{x}^{2}z+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =2{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}(2z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360583 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360583 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95174" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360583 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360583 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $h(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2}{z}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360583 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7a6